{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "07-church.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "Church encodings",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Build booleans, numbers and pairs out of functions, by asking what each one is for.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Lesson 1 promised that functions are enough, yet there is no $\\mathsf{true}$ here and no $3$. To build them, ask what you **do** with a thing, and let the thing be that doing: a thing is what you can do with it.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Booleans choose",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "What do you do with a boolean? Choose: \"if $b$ then $x$ else $y$\". So let the boolean be the chooser: $b\\ x\\ y$ should be $x$ when $b$ is true, and $y$ when it is false.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> Write $\\mathsf{true}$ and $\\mathsf{false}$: each takes two arguments.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Church booleans\n> $\\mathsf{true} = \\lambda t.\\, \\lambda f.\\, t$ keeps the first, and $\\mathsf{false} = \\lambda t.\\, \\lambda f.\\, f$ the second. Then $\\mathsf{if}\\ b\\ x\\ y$ is just $b\\ x\\ y$, and $\\mathsf{not}\\ b = b\\ \\mathsf{false}\\ \\mathsf{true}$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The library has `true`, `false`, `if`, `not`, `and`, `or`. Names are unfolded in one δ-step; the result is read back when it is a boolean or a numeral.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "if true a b",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "$\\mathsf{and}\\ p\\ q$ is \"if $p$ then $q$ else false\"; the library writes the \"false\" as $p$, which is false in that branch: $\\lambda p.\\, \\lambda q.\\, p\\ q\\ p$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "and true false",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A definition, `name := term`, is there for the cells after it. Exclusive or: if $p$, the opposite of $q$, else $q$:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "xor := λp. λq. p (not q) q",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "xor true false",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "xor true true",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "$\\mathsf{false}$ and $0$ (below) are the same term, $\\lambda t.\\, \\lambda f.\\, f$: the reading names it as the numeral.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Numerals iterate",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "What do you do with a natural number $n$? Do something $n$ times. So let $n$ be that: given $f$ and $x$, it applies $f$ to $x$, $n$ times.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Church numerals\n> $n$ is the function that applies $f$ to $x$ $n$ times: $0 = \\lambda f.\\, \\lambda x.\\, x$, $1 = \\lambda f.\\, \\lambda x.\\, f\\ x$, $2 = \\lambda f.\\, \\lambda x.\\, f\\ (f\\ x)$, and so on. In a λ-term a digit is its numeral.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "λf. λx. f (f (f x))",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> Write $\\mathsf{succ}$: given $n$, apply $f$ once more than $n$ does. Then $\\mathsf{add}\\ m\\ n$: apply $f$ $n$ times, then $m$ more.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "$n\\ f\\ x$ is $f$ applied $n$ times, so $\\mathsf{succ} = \\lambda n.\\, \\lambda f.\\, \\lambda x.\\, f\\ (n\\ f\\ x)$, and $\\mathsf{add} = \\lambda m.\\, \\lambda n.\\, \\lambda f.\\, \\lambda x.\\, m\\ f\\ (n\\ f\\ x)$: start from $n\\ f\\ x$ and apply $f$ $m$ more times. ($\\lambda n.\\, \\lambda f.\\, \\lambda x.\\, n\\ f\\ (f\\ x)$, one extra $f$ first instead of last, is a successor just as good.) These are the library's:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "succ 2",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "add 2 3",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Multiplying is iterating an iteration: $\\mathsf{mul}\\ m\\ n = \\lambda f.\\, m\\ (n\\ f)$ does \"$f$, $n$ times\" $m$ times. And $\\mathsf{pow}\\ m\\ n = n\\ m$: $n$ copies of \"do it $m$ times\", composed.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "mul 2 3",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "pow 2 3",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "So a function that applies its argument twice is the numeral 2, and applying it to itself applies four times:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "twice := λf. λx. f (f x)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "twice twice succ 0",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The predecessor: a puzzle",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Subtracting one looks easy and is not. A numeral can apply $f$; it can never undo it. Church himself at first doubted it could be done; his student Kleene found how.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> How do you get $n - 1$ from something that can only go forward $n$ times? A hint: go forward, but carry where you were one step ago.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Carrying two numbers needs a pair, and a pair is what you do with it: hand both to a selector.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Pairs\n> $\\mathsf{pair}\\ a\\ b = \\lambda s.\\, s\\ a\\ b$ holds $a$ and $b$ until a selector comes: $\\mathsf{fst}\\ p = p\\ \\mathsf{true}$ and $\\mathsf{snd}\\ p = p\\ \\mathsf{false}$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "fst (pair a b)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Kleene's trick: start at $(0, 0)$ and step $(a, b) \\mapsto (b, b + 1)$, so $(0, 0) \\to (0, 1) \\to (1, 2) \\to (2, 3) \\to \\cdots$. The first place lags one behind the second, so after $n \\geq 1$ steps it holds $n - 1$; with no steps it holds $0$, which is as close as the naturals get to $0 - 1$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "pred := λn. fst (n (λp. pair (snd p) (succ (snd p))) (pair 0 0))",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "pred 3",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Subtraction is then a predecessor iterated: $\\mathsf{sub}\\ m\\ n$ applies $\\mathsf{pred}$ to $m$, $n$ times. And $\\mathsf{iszero}\\ n = n\\ (\\lambda y.\\, \\mathsf{false})\\ \\mathsf{true}$: one application of \"make it false\" is enough to spoil $\\mathsf{true}$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "sub := λm. λn. n pred m",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "sub 3 1",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "iszero (pred 1)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Lists fold",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "What do you do with a list? Walk it: combine each head with the result for the rest, and say what to give for the empty list. So a list is its own fold.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Church lists\n> Given what to do with a head and the rest ($c$) and what to give for the empty list ($n$), a list does it: $\\mathsf{nil} = \\lambda c.\\, \\lambda n.\\, n$ and $\\mathsf{cons}\\ h\\ t = \\lambda c.\\, \\lambda n.\\, c\\ h\\ (t\\ c\\ n)$, so $[1, 2]$ is $\\lambda c.\\, \\lambda n.\\, c\\ 1\\ (c\\ 2\\ n)$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "nil := λc. λn. n",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "cons := λh. λt. λc. λn. c h (t c n)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "($\\mathsf{nil}$ is $\\lambda c.\\, \\lambda n.\\, n$ again: the same term as $0$ and $\\mathsf{false}$.) Folding with $\\mathsf{add}$ from $0$ sums a list, and counting the heads gives its length:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "cons 1 (cons 2 nil) add 0",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "length := λl. l (λh. λr. succ r) 0",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "length (cons a (cons b (cons c nil)))",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "In Lean",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Lean is typed, so a Church numeral is used at one type at a time; inside that type it is iteration.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "/-- The Church numeral n at one type: apply f to x, n times. -/\ndef church {α : Type} : Nat → (α → α) → α → α\n  | 0, _, x => x\n  | n + 1, f, x => f (church n f x)\n\n#eval church 3 (· + 1) 0\n#eval church 3 (· ++ \"!\") \"hi\"",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem church_add {α : Type} (f : α → α) (x : α) : ∀ m n, church (m + n) f x = church m f (church n f x) := by",
      "type": "exercise",
      "lean": true,
      "prompt": "Adding numerals is composing: $m + n$ applications are $n$ and then $m$ more. Prove it by induction on $m$.",
      "leanSolution": "  intro m n\n  induction m with\n  | zero => simp [church]\n  | succ k ih => rw [Nat.succ_add]; simp [church, ih]",
      "hints": [
        "`intro m n`, then `induction m with`.",
        "In the successor case `(k + 1) + n` is not syntactically `(k + n) + 1`: rewrite with `Nat.succ_add` first, then `simp [church, ih]`."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Exercises",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Answer with a numeral or a boolean by name, or with the λ-term. A cell that starts with a digit is read as arithmetic, so the last two questions name their strategy, `normal:`.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "pred 2",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Reduce $\\mathsf{pred}\\ 2$.",
      "hints": [
        "Two steps of the pair counter: $(0, 0) \\to (0, 1) \\to (1, 2)$."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "or false (not false)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Reduce $\\mathsf{or}\\ \\mathsf{false}\\ (\\mathsf{not}\\ \\mathsf{false})$.",
      "hints": [
        "$\\mathsf{not}\\ \\mathsf{false}$ is $\\mathsf{true}$."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "snd (pair 1 (succ 1))",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Reduce $\\mathsf{snd}\\ (\\mathsf{pair}\\ 1\\ (\\mathsf{succ}\\ 1))$.",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "cons 1 (cons 1 (cons 1 nil)) add 0",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Sum the list $[1, 1, 1]$: reduce $\\mathsf{cons}\\ 1\\ (\\mathsf{cons}\\ 1\\ (\\mathsf{cons}\\ 1\\ \\mathsf{nil}))\\ \\mathsf{add}\\ 0$.",
      "hints": [
        "The list puts $\\mathsf{add}$ between its elements and $0$ at the end: $1 + (1 + (1 + 0))$."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "normal: 3 not true",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "A numeral is a loop. Reduce $3\\ \\mathsf{not}\\ \\mathsf{true}$.",
      "hints": [
        "It applies $\\mathsf{not}$ to $\\mathsf{true}$ three times."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "normal: 2 (add 3) 0",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Reduce $2\\ (\\mathsf{add}\\ 3)\\ 0$. Which operation on 2 and 3 have you invented?",
      "hints": [
        "It applies \"add 3\" to 0, twice."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> A thing is what you can do with it: booleans choose, numerals iterate, pairs wait for a selector, lists are their own folds. Arithmetic is iteration of iterations, and the predecessor is a counter carried in a pair.\n\nEvery loop here is a numeral, which knows in advance how many times to run. A loop that runs until it is done needs a function that calls itself. With no names, how can a function refer to itself? Lesson 8.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
